# ask: how · new
# Why this picture: the idea lives in the array itself, so the array is the stage; pointers are its moving parts.
title "Binary search"
mark a array items=[2,5,8,12,16,23,38,56,72] label="sorted, looking for 23"
mark lo label text="lo" at=a[0]
mark hi label text="hi" at=a[8]
Say: "We want twenty-three. The list is sorted, so every look can rule out half of it."
# The middle is a place, so a pointer moves there; {a[4]} speaks the value the stage shows.
Say: "Check the |m|middle: {a[4]}."
@m mark mid label text="mid" at=a[4]
# "Rule out" is a claim the picture must carry: the ruled-out cells go grey on the word.
Say: "{a[4]} is too small, and so is everything |left|left of it. Move lo |past|past it."
@left mark a colors=[grey,grey,grey,grey,grey,-,-,-,-]
@past mark lo at=a[5]
Say: "New middle: |m|{a[6]}. Too big, so hi comes |back|back, and the right side is out too."
@m mark mid at=a[6]
@back mark hi at=a[5]
with mark a colors=[grey,grey,grey,grey,grey,-,grey,grey,grey]
# The answer is the one cell left lit; focus says "this one" without new marks.
Say: "Lo and hi meet on |found|{a[5]}. Three looks instead of six."
@found mark mid at=a[5]
with focus a[5]
Say: "Each look halves what's left: a thousand items take only about ten looks."
